/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The integral diverges"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{e}^{u}]_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/3df1853e93a5ab2a1c1ca4114d450b29a69223bd576b059d9aef1b17c68fe5b7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a15390178eee87ca05d2f3502c32a44baa8c6e4266127e91909847ae46cbc72" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{e}^{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2{e}^{2}}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The integral diverges"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/state.json" "file_sha256" "03e08d0b709381ddb0be2784c6d272e736fe12b64fff6dfa10bd129efa1200b2" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10554.json" "file_sha256" "4ceab7adbea10920a3a764828778c21e3a219ed512a5cb911e16119198901a38" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10554.json" "file_sha256" "4ceab7adbea10920a3a764828778c21e3a219ed512a5cb911e16119198901a38" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10764.json" "file_sha256" "774e5a27591dd17e29660305ccf486074ae0c38eee607c0ca0e4596c027f3e02" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10764.json" "file_sha256" "774e5a27591dd17e29660305ccf486074ae0c38eee607c0ca0e4596c027f3e02" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10553.json" "file_sha256" "688979b59094104ff092bd09c8e585129a32bb58ea9306928e901e74947bf438" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/example-10553.json" "file_sha256" "688979b59094104ff092bd09c8e585129a32bb58ea9306928e901e74947bf438" "basis" 1568 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-152880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{2}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot ({e}^{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot ({e}^{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-152880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{e}^{2}"] ["math" "6e"] ["math" "\\frac{3e}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{e}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-20875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-20875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{2x}]}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/b350aaef1440524297a84168de46be1171b7686d9937ef5bbfc5cd55835ea54f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-20875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-20875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-20875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-20875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-350831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-350831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-5x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-5a}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-5a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{-5x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-5x}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-5x}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/5010180e9cd41608abc630cd8974cc69ee78337bcb46a7f079d58eeb8dda9cc2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-350831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-350831" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-350831" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{4}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/115e3198226940a76b770f5942bb06e6496bd234d6cb5fee2d33f19038931bd3.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot ({e}^{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =5({e}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-152882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5{e}^{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5e}"] ["math" "\\frac{5e}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{{e}^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{4}}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5{e}^{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-346606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-346606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-2}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-346606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-346606" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-346606" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4e^{-2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-3x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3a}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/998ec723fd7212da4382718750465ce313349ed3f2c0258cc1f68e0a5485d968.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{3a}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-351559" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351559" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{24\\ln(2)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(8-{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/c2af905a46cea7b7512a727a5e53a2a178e4902f7114f86439365684e6685efa.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-351416" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351416" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{3\\ln(2)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{6}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{6}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({6}^{0}-{6}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{6}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={6}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{6}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{6}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/2ebee84cf543b25e56b360f145459696554a3b67daaf64c21812cb4b92fa420a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{6}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] ["math" "\\frac{24}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({5}^{1}-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/06c51ecc423683f4377248004b7a318f1348836bd752a7f65f94457d1774e64d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/bdf8e7dad9550977ad6bd207b43cd75e91226ed84c2807dac3d023470d9e02ce.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-152232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{5}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{5}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{5}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/3cb8903ed53a713c5e23e4dbad4801788440954ac3a5f7e08ab1bd71cc9c631f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{5}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-152260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{4}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{4}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/e50ca6e9326f5a6146a7a486adcc2d3d5f5c059eae3e2e1b06fbff8664b1c7df.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-351523" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351523" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\ln(4)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{e}^{u}]_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/3df1853e93a5ab2a1c1ca4114d450b29a69223bd576b059d9aef1b17c68fe5b7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{e}^{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2{e}^{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-152265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/34abda46dc9fc2c6f14ce4f8460ba076a16004bb9141eb8074cdc08e0c12fd2f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-351528" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351528" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}}{\\sqrt{3}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/1c0fc31d392bbaccf9a292553321bd60ccbdb2a02f3675543797f570c4fa0ef1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7daed03fc8c3c14b6f0511b41ff3700c3e15cf58a8b79ca63f477ad77006bf"} {"question" "q-351513" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "q-351513" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/state.json" "file_sha256" "71dadd4ee56a012d608d3586298edc14783b17ad20132873d2cff17676370d64"} {"question" "e-10554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/example-10554.json" "file_sha256" "b1d851fe6d5b1aa5f576ef26b4238520dac2152449c1818b3439d122bb3d07a0"} {"question" "e-10554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/example-10554.json" "file_sha256" "b1d851fe6d5b1aa5f576ef26b4238520dac2152449c1818b3439d122bb3d07a0"} {"question" "e-10764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/example-10764.json" "file_sha256" "643b831a3f39ca0c93b913e838ede588c1ae331e41594633c64f39cbec4e9355"} {"question" "e-10764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/example-10764.json" "file_sha256" "643b831a3f39ca0c93b913e838ede588c1ae331e41594633c64f39cbec4e9355"} {"question" "e-10553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/example-10553.json" "file_sha256" "92e5a3db64d03537f4bba5abe09287a9d9b4e99ea9ef4cd205bc5fa3b7654feb"} {"question" "e-10553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047719/example-10553.json" "file_sha256" "92e5a3db64d03537f4bba5abe09287a9d9b4e99ea9ef4cd205bc5fa3b7654feb"}] "usage" {"q-351513" [] "q-152265" [] "q-351523" [] "q-351416" [] "e-10553" [] "q-152876" [] "e-10764" [] "q-152875" [] "e-10554" []} "basis" 1699 "retractions" [[17592186317075 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(8-{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/c2af905a46cea7b7512a727a5e53a2a178e4902f7114f86439365684e6685efa.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317047 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{6}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{6}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({6}^{0}-{6}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{6}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={6}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{6}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{6}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/2ebee84cf543b25e56b360f145459696554a3b67daaf64c21812cb4b92fa420a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{6}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186301311 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({5}^{1}-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/06c51ecc423683f4377248004b7a318f1348836bd752a7f65f94457d1774e64d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186301359 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{4}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{4}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/e50ca6e9326f5a6146a7a486adcc2d3d5f5c059eae3e2e1b06fbff8664b1c7df.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317040 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{e}^{u}]_{a}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{3}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/3df1853e93a5ab2a1c1ca4114d450b29a69223bd576b059d9aef1b17c68fe5b7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{2+{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({e}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186301356 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}}{\\sqrt{3}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/1c0fc31d392bbaccf9a292553321bd60ccbdb2a02f3675543797f570c4fa0ef1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192257 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192292 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192256 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186301313 17592186301314 17592186301315 17592186301316 17592186301317 17592186301358 17592186301361 17592186301362 17592186317042 17592186317043 17592186317044 17592186317045 17592186317046 17592186317049 17592186317050 17592186317051 17592186317052 17592186317053 17592186317077] "immutable_fields" [[17592186301312 :answer-field/correct] [1759218630131